.TH CBMAP_REDECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_redeclare \- re-establish the companion type variables for an ordered map handle from another scope
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_redeclare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares the companion type variables that carry key_t and val_t for an
ordered map handle that already exists. The macro declares no handle. It
creates no map and it destroys none. It gives back only the type
information that the type-inferred macros need.

Every type-inferred macro gets the key type and the value type of a map from
two ordinary local variables. Those variables carry the name of the handle.
cbmap_construct(3) and cbmap_declare(3) create them beside the handle. They
are local declarations, so they do not travel with the handle to another
function. Inside that function, cbmap_insert(hm_name, ...) and its siblings
name variables that nothing declares there, and the translation unit does
not compile. This macro declares those variables again. Every caller that
passes a cbmap across a function boundary needs this macro.

The types here must be the types that the caller created the map with. The
macros use them to read a key and to size a value. They also use them to
decide whether a value is a char * that the map stores by contents.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name of the map handle that already exists
key_t    Key type that the caller created the map with
val_t    Value type that the caller created the map with
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations. Put it where a declaration
is legal, and give it a semicolon of its own at the end
The declared variables are pointers. Nothing dereferences them at run time.
They exist so that typeof and the type-introspection macros can read the
types from them. They cost nothing in the generated code
The macro declares variables only. This is also the way to reach a handle
that a struct field or a global holds. Name the macro in each function that
works on that handle
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The declared value type can have a width that differs from the stored
width. cbmap_get(3) and cbmap_get_ptr(3) report that at run time. They
describe the size mismatch and stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
But they find no mismatch between two types of the same width. The types
here are therefore a promise that the caller keeps, and not a promise that
the compiler checks. A key of the wrong width gets a report and not an
acceptance. The raw entry points return ccol_invalid_args for it. The order
of the tree has a meaning only for keys of the width that the caller
created the map for.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void bump(cbmap scores, int who) {
    cbmap_redeclare(scores, int, int);
    int *slot = cbmap_get_ptr(scores, who);
    if (slot) {
        (*slot)++;
    }
}

cbmap_construct(scores, int, int);
int who = 7, score = 42;
cbmap_insert(scores, who, score);
bump(scores, 7);
cbmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_get (3),
.BR cbmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR cbmap_insert (3)
